oo. One quick read.\n\nThen write iteration 1 file, delta, gateway, reduce.\n\nLet me also handle Q1's \"per level\": Level 1/2/3 spec.md has problem anchor required; phase level has a 'purpose' gate key in the manifest [spec-kit-docs.json levels.phase.sectionGates includes 'purpose'] — interesting: the phase section-gate key is named 'purpose', not 'problem', while the template anchor is `problem`. That's an inconsistency worth recording as a finding (manifest gate id 'purpose' vs template anchor 'problem'); but since phase is exempt from ANCHORS_VALID, the mismatch is inert. Let me double check the phase gates structure: for level phase, gates keys printed were top-level including 'metadata','purpose','phase-list','phase-governance'. Let me print phase's spec.md gate mapping to see whether 'purpose' maps to the spec.md document. Actually earlier `Object.keys(gates)` printed document names and anchor ids mixed because JSON structure is document->anchor->levels for some, and anchor->levels at top for others? In level 1, keys were spec.md, plan.md, tasks.md, implementation-summary.md, timeline.md, decision-record.md, metadata, problem, scope, requirements, success-criteria, risks, questions, goal.md — that's the union of document names and top-level anchor gates. The structure: sectionGates = { \"spec.md\": {...anchors...}, \"plan.md\": {...}, ..., \"metadata\": [...levels], \"problem\": [...], ... }. So top-level anchor gates are cross-document? Hmm.\n\nLet me print the phase row's sectionGates fully and level 1's top-level anchor gates to understand. Actually, for accuracy: print m.levels.phase.sectionGates with JSON."}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b78e3815dd7980cf","tool":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/371-purpose-retention-research && node -e '\nconst m=require(\"./.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/spec-kit-docs.json\");\nconst g=m.levels.phase.sectionGates;\nfor (const k of Object.keys(g)) console.log(k, \"=>\", JSON.stringify(g[k]));\n'; echo \"=== is-phase-parent ===\"; sed -n '1,60p' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/is-phase-parent.ts","description":"Print phase gates and is-phase-parent logic"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b78e3815dd7980cf","status":"completed","result":"spec.md => {\"metadata\":[\"phase\"],\"problem\":[\"phase\"],\"scope\":[\"phase\"],\"phase-map\":[\"phase\"],\"questions\":[\"phase\"]}\ntimeline.md => {\"metadata\":[\"phase\"],\"timeline\":[\"phase\"],\"milestones\":[\"phase\"]}\ndecision-record.md => {\"adr-001\":[\"phase\"],\"adr-001-context\":[\"phase\"],\"adr-001-decision\":[\"phase\"],\"adr-001-alternatives\":[\"phase\"],\"adr-001-consequences\":[\"phase\"],\"adr-001-five-checks\":[\"phase\"],\"adr-001-impl\":[\"phase\"]}\nmetadata => [\"phase\"]\npurpose => [\"phase\"]\nphase-list => [\"phase\"]\nphase-governance => [\"phase\"]\ngoal.md => {\"directive\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"3+\",\"phase\"],\"completion\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"3+\",\"phase\"],\"log\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"3+\",\"phase\"],\"binding\":[\"phase\"]}\n=== is-phase-parent ===\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Is Phase Parent\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nimport * as fs from 'fs';\nimport * as path from 'path';\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst PHASE_CHILD_REGEX = /^[0-9]{3}-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$/;\n\n// The graph-metadata writer enumerates a spec folder's children by directory\n// name alone — every immediate subdirectory whose name is a spec-leaf segment\n// (three digits, optionally followed by '-' or '_' and more) — and folds them\n// into children_ids by union: it adds derived children and never prunes. This\n// pattern mirrors that writer-side membership rule so a read-only check can\n// predict exactly which children a refresh would add. It is deliberately looser\n// than PHASE_CHILD_REGEX: manifest classification wants the strict slug shape,\n// but child-drift detection must match whatever the writer would actually derive\n// (including bare-number and underscore folders the strict rule excludes).\nconst DERIVED_CHILD_REGEX = /^[0-9]{3}(?:[-_].+)?$/;\n\n// Thresholds for phase-parent manifest health. Mirrors the\n// authoritative copy in runtime/lib/spec/is-phase-parent.ts so shell rules\n// and TypeScript runtime callers see identical buckets.\nexport const PHASE_PARENT_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 20;\nexport const PHASE_PARENT_ERROR_THRESHOLD = 40;\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 3. TYPE DEFINITIONS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n/** Advisory status bucket for a phase-parent folder's direct child count. */\nexport type PhaseParentHealthStatus = 'ok' | 'warning' | 'error' | 'not_phase_parent';\n\n/** Advisory health summary for a candidate phase-parent folder. */\nexport interface PhaseParentHealth {\n  childCount: number;\n  status: PhaseParentHealthStatus;\n  recommendation: string;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 4. GENERATOR HARDENING\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n/**\n * Return true when a folder has at least one direct phase child with spec metadata.\n */\n// The canonical classifier in the runtime reads the same child enumeration the\n// graph-metadata writer maps when generator hardening is on (its default), so a\n// populated bare-number or underscore child counts there. This entrypoint keeps\n// no runtime import, so it mirrors that rule: under hardening, membership is the\n// writer's leaf-segment shape; with the flag explicitly off, the strict slug.\n"}
