{"type":"thinking","text":"Good characterization: the 22 handovers' \"purpose\" mentions are incidental — checklists referencing spec.md (\"Spec file: spec.md (root purpose and the phase map)\"), a trap note, a frontmatter description field (\"purpose: Resume document for...\"), and an \"Objective:\" line in one handover (009.../005-sk-deep-research-review-mode line 33: \"**Objective:** Deep research on improving ... then implement all recommendations\"). That last one is a genuine instance of a purpose restated in a handover — a counterexample worth citing: one of 97 restates an objective, as a bare line outside any schema.\n\nSo Q3 answer:\n- Across the live corpus (97 handovers): 0 have a Purpose heading; 22 contain purpose/problem/objective strings, mostly incidental (references to spec.md, a trap note, frontmatter description); exactly one restates an objective as a bare \"**Objective:**\" line [SOURCE: handover.md:33 in that packet].\n- Continuity blocks: 0 of the implementation-summary continuity blocks carry a purpose/goal/objective/problem key across all files (rg 0 hits).\n- Purpose is authored on spec.md: 3211/3294 live spec.md carry the problem anchor; purpose filled 2943 (89.3%), placeholder 22, empty 246.\n- Phase children drift: parent 001-system-speckit-v4 purpose 481 chars; 69 children, 65 with purpose text; only 16 share ≥3 significant words with parent's purpose; lexical probe.\n\nAlso: 68/97 handover packets also have implementation-summary.md — \"several continuity saves\" surface exists, yet neither carries purpose.\n\nNow write iteration 3. Findings (7):\n\n1. Purpose is authored and mostly filled at spec level: of 3294 live spec.md (excluding _archive/z_archive), 3211 carry the problem anchor; 2943 have filled Purpose text (89.3%), 22 still placeholder, 246 have the anchor but an empty Purpose subsection. [command measured]\n2. Handovers do not restate purpose: 0 of 97 handover.md has a Purpose heading; 22 mention purpose/problem/objective, and inspection shows these are incidental (checklist reference to spec.md, a trap-table note, a frontmatter description), with one genuine restatement: an `**Objective:**` line [SOURCE: that handover:33].\n3. Continuity state never carries purpose: 0 hits for a purpose/goal/objective/problem key at continuity indentation across every implementation-summary.md in specs/ (rg measured). Combined with iteration 2's field list, this is absence by construction.\n4. 68 of 97 handover packets also carry implementation-summary.md, so the \"several continuity saves\" case is common; both surfaces are purpose-free.\n5. Phase children drift from the parent purpose: measured on the largest live phase parent (69 children); only 16 of 65 child purposes share ≥3 significant words with the parent's 481-char purpose; examples: child 001 purpose is about preflight helper targeting, child 002 about the external-MCP guard (lexical overlap 0), while child 003 quotes the parent note (21).\n6. The parent's purpose is itself a *container* purpose (hold the 38 packets as phases in chronological order...), not the user need of any child; so even a child that restates it would not inherit the user need.\n7. In the handover that does restate an objective, the line lives outside every schema field, so no reader parses it (iteration 2 finding 6/9 prove the parser reads four labelled fields) — purpose presence in handovers is author will, not mechanism.\n\nWait, finding 6: the parent purpose text is \"Hold the thirty-eight packets as phases in chronological order under one parent that a reader can call v4, with a timeline, literal names, and retrieval that re...\" — that's a parent-level purpose (what the decomposition achieves), consistent with the phase-parent template note. Good. Note \"thirty-eight\" vs 69 children counted — the purpose text says 38 while there are 69 child dirs; that's a drift signal itself (parent's stated count stale). Nice detail: the parent purpose claims 38 packets but the folder holds 69 child dirs. That's evidence of parent drift over time. I should double check the count: earlier output said \"children: 69\". And the purpose says \"thirty-eight packets\". Strong finding: the parent's own purpose text is stale relative to its phases.\n\nHmm, but \"children: 69\" includes only dirs matching ^\\d{3}-; find earlier said 70 (maybe including a non-conforming dir). Good: parent purpose says 38; 69 numbered children exist.\n\nAlso for Q3 the question asks \"is the purpose restated, shortened or absent?\" Answer: absent on hop surfaces; authored on spec.md; children restate their own local purpose, not the parent's.\n\nnewInfoRatio: all new → 1.0. Q3 answered.\n\nLet me write iteration 3 and record. Also note edge case: ambiguous measurement (lexical overlap is a proxy). edgeCase: \"ambiguous-input\"? Better: \"ambiguous-input\" isn't right. Use \"none\" and describe the proxy limitation in the narrative. Actually the enum choices: none, ambiguous-input, contradictory-evidence, missing-dependency, partial-success. The lexical probe is an inference limitation, not an edge case. I'll use \"none\" and mark the inference explicitly.\n\nClock now ~21:09. Let me write."}
