{"type":"thinking","text":"Good: 4 rows (run_initialized + 3 iterations). min iterations 3 reached; but stopPolicy max-iterations means continue to 5.\n\nIteration 4: Q4 — which candidate change closes the measured gap at the lowest cost?\n\nCandidates to test:\nA) Parent-level acceptance criteria (operator candidate).\nB) Parent-level user story or purpose/problem section (operator candidate) — note the parent already HAS problem/purpose; so this candidate's evidence: already present in template, so it doesn't close the hop gap; would need to make it survive hops.\nC) Improved parent-specific spec template (operator candidate) — e.g., add user story/purpose to phase-parent template; but same issue: it's an authoring surface, not a hop carrier.\nD) A purpose field in the continuity block (thin record) — evidence-supported alternative. Cost: edit thin-continuity-record schema + validator + generate-context payload + writer + resume parser + facet builder + template. Compare.\nE) A purpose line in the compaction brief (session_state / facet) — could read spec.md or the goal objective at PreCompact. Cost: compact-inject adds a read of the packet's purpose (spec.md problem anchor or goal.md objective), inject 1 line into the brief. But it requires reading packet docs in the hook (currently doesn't).\nF) A purpose row in the resume brief (ladder signal field + parser + presentation) — modest cost: ResumeSignal + parse from spec.md problem anchor (which is already a stable heading) + template row.\n\nThe lowest-cost candidate that closes the measured gap: since the purpose exists on spec.md and the gap is on hop surfaces, the smallest change is to read the existing purpose at the hop (not add a new authoring surface). Concretely: add a purpose line to the resume brief/compaction facet sourced from spec.md's `problem` anchor Purpose subsection or goal.md's Objective.\n\nWhich is lowest cost? Evaluate by counting files that must change for each candidate. Let me measure the cost precisely:\n- Candidate D (continuity field `purpose`): files: thin-continuity-record.ts (interface + validation + compact serialization + facets), generate-context.ts (payload schema/validation), implementation-summary.md.tmpl (template field), spec-doc-structure.ts maybe continuity schema, resume-ladder.ts (parse continuity signal), plus tests (MEMORY_0NN validation). Could be ~6-8 files + tests + migration for existing packets without the field.\n- Candidate F (purpose in resume brief + compaction facet): resume-ladder.ts (signal shape + spec-doc parser), speckit-resume-presentation.txt (brief row), thin-continuity-record.ts (facet builder, optional), compact-inject.ts (read spec.md or call ladder). ~3-4 files.\n- Candidate A (parent-level acceptance criteria): acceptance-criteria.md is optional at parent level, and a parent AC would add a new required doc at parent level → cost: manifest lazy/required change, create.sh seeding, AC_CLOSURE rule scope, template. And it wouldn't carry purpose anyway (no purpose column) — so it fails the gap test.\n- Candidate B: parent already has Problem & Purpose (phase-parent template), so adding it = no-op; what's missing is a *reader*. Fails the gap test at zero cost but no benefit.\n- Candidate C: improving the parent template cannot help because the parent template already has the section and the validator exempts parents — the gap is at the hop, not authoring.\n\nSo the recommendation: smallest change = one purpose line sourced from the existing authored purpose at the two read surfaces: the compaction facet and the resume brief; minimal version = only the resume brief + compaction brief reading spec.md's `### Purpose` (or goal.md Objective when present).\n\nBut I need evidence for cost claims:\n- Where would resume-ladder get purpose? It already reads `spec.md` in the spec-docs tier (SPEC_DOC_PRIORITY includes spec.md), and the handover parser already reads labelled values. So the smallest change: extract the Purpose subsection in parseSpecDocumentSignal / add a `purpose` field to ResumeSignal, surface it in the brief. But note: when a handover exists, the ladder never reads spec.md (it picks handover as primary). So a purpose field would be absent in the common case unless the ladder also reads spec.md as a *tertiary* source for purpose. That's an important cost detail: the ladder's tiers are exclusive-by-priority; adding purpose from spec.md requires a separate read even when handover wins. Hmm — actually buildResumeLadder reads all snapshots? It reads handover snapshot, continuity snapshot, specDocSnapshots; primary selection then synthesize. The spec-doc signal is computed (line 1141) even if handover wins, but only used as fallback/secondary. So extending the synthesized result to include a purpose from the spec-doc snapshot is a small change in synthesizeResult + parser.\n- For compaction: the hook would need to open spec.md (it currently never does). That's a new read in a hook with a deadline (2.8s budget, hookLog warns over 1500ms). Cost: a file read is trivial; but the hook needs the spec folder — it detects \"Active spec folder\" only from the transcript; it doesn't know the packet path reliably. That's the real cost: the compaction brief has no reliable packet pointer, so it cannot read spec.md unless the pointer is recorded somewhere (e.g., last spec folder state readLastSpecFolder in pi/devin handlers).\n\nThat's a key insight: pi/devin handlers call `readLastSpecFolder(ctx.cwd, sessionId)` (hook state) — so there IS a mechanism to know the last spec folder (hook-state). Claude's compact-inject instead parses the transcript for the spec folder. So a purpose line in the compaction brief is feasible when a spec folder is resolvable.\n\nLet me measure these cost surfaces:\n1. `readLastSpecFolder` existence + where last spec folder is persisted (hooks/claude/hook-state.ts + spec-gate writes it?). \n2. The resume ladder's ordering: confirm spec-doc snapshot is computed regardless (lines 1128-1141).\n3. The number of files that would change for a purpose field: count validation constants MEMORY_0NN in thin-continuity-record (maybe a fixed schema list) and generate-context's payload validation.\n\nAlso test operator candidate C by checking create.sh's phase-parent seeding: does it scaffold acceptance-criteria.md for parents? `--level phase`? Let me check whether parent gets acceptance-criteria.md at all (optional addon only at L2/3; phase level requiredCoreDocs = spec.md; optional = []). So parent-level acceptance criteria would require a manifest change + AC_CLOSURE scope change — measurable cost: 3 files (manifest, create.sh, AC_CLOSURE rule) + template additions.\n\nLet me gather: hook-state last spec folder; resume ladder's spec-doc computation; manifest/edit points for parent AC (ac-closure rule source). Also count the MEMORY_0NN validation functions and where generate-context documents payload fields (already have :170-181).\n\nLet me do 3-4 targeted greps."}
