{"type":"thinking","text":"Excellent:\n- 319 phase parents (live); 94 carry goal.md (~29%); 10 carry acceptance-criteria.md (~3%).\n- goal.md is referenced by 0 hooks, 0 resume-ladder, 0 continuity lib. So the durable directive is never read by any hop — a purpose carrier with no reader.\n\nThat's a powerful finding for Q4: the purpose already exists at parent level in two machine-readable places (spec.md `### Purpose`, goal.md `### DURABLE DIRECTIVE` Objective), and the gap is that no hop reads either. Therefore candidate changes that ADD an authoring surface cannot close the measured gap; the lowest-cost change is at a read surface.\n\nCost ranking with measured counts:\n- F (resume brief + ladder): touches 2 files (resume-ladder.ts, speckit-resume-presentation.txt) + optionally thin-continuity-record.ts for facets; no schema change; no migration; covers every runtime's recovery because all point to /speckit:resume.\n- E (compaction brief): touches compact-inject.ts (+ shared if dsh) — but needs packet resolution; lastSpecFolder exists in hook state (claude/hook-state.ts:345; session-stop persists it), so one read; 1-2 files. But only Claude/codex/dsh paths; pi/devin would need their own.\n- D (continuity purpose field): 4+ files + validation rule + template + migration of 4,657 summaries; highest cost.\n- A (parent AC): 10/319 parents already have it; template has no purpose column; requires manifest + AC_CLOSURE scoping + create.sh + template = 4+ files and still no purpose carrier → fails the gap test.\n- B (parent user story/purpose section): already present in phase-parent template (problem+purpose) → no-op for authoring; fails gap test unless a hop reads it.\n- C (improved parent template): adds user story to parent; still no reader → fails gap test alone; can be bundled with F at marginal cost (one template block) but is not the closing change.\n\nRecommendation: F as the single smallest change that closes the measured gap, specifically: add `purpose` to ResumeSignal, populated from (a) goal.md `DURABLE DIRECTIVE` Objective when present, else (b) spec.md `### Purpose`; render it as one `Purpose:` row in the RESUME BRIEF; and (optional second half) feed the same string into the \"Goal\" facet instead of next_safe_action (renaming the semantic). Actually careful: replacing the Goal facet content would be a behavior change; better: add a `purpose` facet/row. Keep recommendation minimal: one row in the brief.\n\nValidation plan: \n- unit: resume-ladder.vitest.ts fixture with a packet spec.md purpose and a goal.md objective → assert signal.purpose and brief row;\n- manual: run /speckit:resume against a packet whose purpose is filled and confirm the row;\n- negative: packet with no purpose → row omitted, not \"None\".\n\nAlso cost: the ladder already reads spec.md in the spec-doc tier (line 1128-1141), so extraction is a parser addition; goal.md is not read at all, so reading it is one more file read per resume.\n\nHmm — which is cheaper: source from spec.md (already read) or goal.md (not read)? spec.md is already in the snapshot set, so zero extra I/O. But the ladder's spec-doc tier is skipped when handover exists? The snapshots are still read (1128-1141 computed unconditionally). So purpose from spec.md is nearly free. goal.md adds I/O + priority slot. So F's cheapest form: extract `### Purpose` from the already-read spec.md snapshot, add to ResumeSignal + brief. That's 2 files. \n\nFor phase parents: spec.md purpose exists in parents too (65/69 children etc.; parents have purpose filled presumably). Good.\n\nI'll also note the parent-level container-purpose issue (iteration 3 finding 7): for a phase child resume, spec.md is the child's — so the child's local purpose surfaces, which is right; the parent's container purpose stays at the parent. That's fine.\n\nNow: newInfoRatio for iteration 4: findings are new (cost/coverage measurements, goal.md unread) → 1.0? Some are synthesis of prior evidence. I'd say ~0.75: some findings new (goal.md read count 0, 94/319 goal.md, 10/319 AC, lastSpecFolder mechanism), plus +0.10 simplicity bonus for ranking. Let me compute honestly: 6 findings, of which 4 fully new, 2 partially new (derived) → (4 + 1)/6 = 0.833 + 0.1 bonus = 0.93, capped 1.0 → 0.93. Let me decide findings count as I write.\n\nLet me write iteration 4 with the recommendation, cost, validation.\n\nFindings (7):\n1. The purpose already exists at parent level in two machine-readable carriers: the phase-parent template's PROBLEM & PURPOSE (declared required but unexecuted, iteration 1) and goal.md's DURABLE DIRECTIVE Objective. In the live corpus 319 phase parents exist, 94 carry goal.md, 10 carry acceptance-criteria.md (measured).\n2. No hop reads goal.md: 0 references in resume-ladder, 0 in runtime/hooks, 0 in continuity lib (measured). So the durable directive is authored but has no reader — the gap is on the read side.\n3. Candidate A (parent-level acceptance criteria) fails the gap test and costs more: the AC template has no purpose column (iteration 1), only 10/319 parents have the file, and the phase level's optionalAddonDocs is empty, so enabling it needs the manifest, the AC_CLOSURE rule scope, create.sh seeding and the template (4+ edit points) for an artifact that carries no purpose.\n4. Candidate B (parent-level user story or purpose/problem section) is a no-op: the phase-parent template already has Problem & Purpose [phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl:63-72]; adding it again changes nothing and crosses no hop. It becomes useful only if a hop reads it.\n5. Candidate C (improved parent-specific template) alone cannot close the gap for the same reason: the parent template already carries the section and its validator is exempt; the missing piece is a reader. A user-story block could ride along only as part of a read-side change.\n6. Candidate D (purpose field in the continuity block) is the most expensive: it needs the thin-record schema + validation + compact serializer, the save writer's payload contract, the template, and the resume parser, plus a migration/fallback for the 4,657 existing implementation-summary.md files, and it still leaves handover-only packets (29 without implementation-summary) uncovered.\n7. Candidate E (purpose line in the compaction brief) is feasible and cheap on Claude: the active packet is resolvable without the transcript because `lastSpecFolder` is persisted in hook state [hook-state.ts:345], and the purpose is one file read; but it covers only the compaction hop and needs per-runtime wiring (pi/devin compose their own).\n8. Recommendation (lowest cost, closes the measured gap): add a `purpose` field to the resume ladder's signal and one `Purpose:` row to the RESUME BRIEF, populated from the already-read spec.md `### Purpose` (falling back to an author-labelled line such as `**Objective:**` in a handover). Cost: 2 files (resume-ladder.ts, speckit-resume-presentation.txt) plus one test; no schema change, no migration, no new authoring. Because every runtime's recovery pointer lands on /speckit:resume (iteration 2 finding 5/4), this one read-side change covers every hop the operator described.\n9. Validation step: unit test in resume-ladder.vitest.ts with a fixture packet whose spec.md purpose is filled (assert signal.purpose and the brief row), a second fixture with goal.md only, and a negative fixture with no purpose (assert the row is omitted, not \"None\"); manual check by running /speckit:resume on a packet such as spec 006 itself and confirming the purpose row.\n10. Scope note: the recommendation deliberately does not add a parent-level artifact, because the measurements show parent-level purpose is already authored (94 goal.md, 319 parents) and unread; the bottleneck is the read surface.\n\nThat's 10 findings — good.\n\nnewInfoRatio: 6 new + 4 partial? Let's compute: findings 1,2,3,4? Some are new measurements (1,2), cost synthesis (3,4,5,6,7) partially new, recommendation (8,9,10) synthesis. I'll say fully new = 4 (1,2,8,9), partially new = 6 → (4+3)/10 = 0.70, +0.10 simplicity bonus = 0.80. Justify: \"4 of 10 findings are new measurements, 6 partially new synthesis; the +0.10 bonus applies because the ranking resolves the operator's candidates","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-8ad6869a7ec333cf","tool":"write","input":{"content":"# Iteration 4: Which candidate closes the measured gap at the lowest cost\n\n## Focus\nQuestion 4 of section 7: which candidate change closes the measured gap at the lowest cost. The operator's three candidates are hypotheses to test, not conclusions: (a) acceptance criteria at the parent spec level, (b) a user story or purpose-and-problem section at the parent level, (c) an improved parent-specific spec template. This iteration adds two evidence-supported candidates — a purpose field in the continuity block, and a purpose line in the compaction/resume surfaces — and ranks all five against the gap measured in iterations 1-3.\n\nNo `steer.md` exists in the lineage directory.\n\n## Actions Taken\n1. Measured parent-level artifact coverage: counted live phase parents (folders with two or more `NNN-*` children) and how many carry `goal.md` and `acceptance-criteria.md`.\n2. Grepped for any reader of `goal.md` in the resume ladder, the hook tree and the continuity library.\n3. Located the mechanism that lets a hook resolve the active packet without reading the transcript (`lastSpecFolder` in hook state).\n4. Measured the ladder's read order to establish that the spec-doc snapshots are read even when a handover wins.\n5. Counted edit points each candidate must touch, from the files already read in iterations 1-3.\n\n## Findings\n1. Parent-level purpose is already authored in two machine-readable carriers, and neither is the artifact the operator proposed. The phase-parent template carries `## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE` with Problem Statement and Purpose [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/packet-types/phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl:63-72], and `goal.md` carries `## 1. DURABLE DIRECTIVE` with a one-sentence `**Objective:**` [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/goal.md.tmpl:38-41]. In the live corpus there are 319 phase parents, of which 94 carry `goal.md` and only 10 carry `acceptance-criteria.md` [INFERENCE: measured by the parent walk described in Actions Taken, this iteration].\n2. No hop reads `goal.md`. References to `goal.md` number 0 in `resume-ladder.ts`, 0 across `runtime/hooks/**`, and 0 in `runtime/lib/continuity/**` [INFERENCE: grep counts run this iteration over those three trees]. The durable directive is an authored purpose carrier with no reader, which places the gap on the read side rather than the authoring side.\n3. Candidate (a), parent-level acceptance criteria, fails the gap test and costs the most. `acceptance-criteria.md` has no purpose column (AC-ID, REQ, Given/When/Then, Verification, Status, Waiver) [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl:57-66]; the phase level declares no optional addon docs, so enabling the file at parent level needs the level manifest, the `AC_CLOSURE` rule scope, `create.sh` seeding and the template — four or more edit points [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/spec-kit-docs.json:levels.phase.optionalAddonDocs; .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/rules/check-ac-closure.sh]. Only 10 of 319 parents carry the file today.\n4. Candidate (b), a parent-level user story or purpose-and-problem section, is a no-op as written: the phase-parent template already has Problem & Purpose [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/packet-types/phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl:63-72], and iteration 1 measured that the phase validator exempts parents rather than requiring the section [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/validation/orchestrator.ts:963-970]. Adding the section again changes no artifact and crosses no hop; it becomes useful only when a hop reads it.\n5. Candidate (c), an improved parent-specific template, cannot close the gap on its own for the same reason: the parent template already carries the purpose and the missing piece is a reader. A user-story block could ride along in the same edit as a read-side change, but as a standalone candidate it changes authoring only.\n6. Candidate (d), a purpose field in the continuity block, is the most expensive. It needs the thin-record interface and its `MEMORY_0NN` validation, the compact serializer, the save writer's documented payload contract, the implementation-summary template and the resume parser, plus a fallback story for the 4,657 existing `implementation-summary.md` files that have no such field [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/continuity/thin-continuity-record.ts:60-78; .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/continuity/generate-context.ts:170-181]. It also leaves the 29 handover-only packets uncovered.\n7. Candidate (e), a purpose line in the compaction brief, is feasible and cheap on Claude: the active packet is resolvable without the transcript because `lastSpecFolder` is persisted in hook state [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/claude/hook-state.ts:345], so one extra file read supplies the purpose. It covers only the compaction hop, and pi/devin compose their own sections, so it needs per-runtime wiring [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/devin/post-compaction.cjs:270-290].\n8. Candidate (f), one purpose row on the resume surface, is the smallest change that closes the measured gap. The ladder already reads `spec.md` among its spec-doc snapshots before it chooses a primary tier [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/resume/resume-ladder.ts:1128-1141], and its signal shape is one interface plus one renderer [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/resume/resume-ladder.ts:56-72; .skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-resume-presentation.txt:116-129]. Cost: two files and one test — add `purpose` to the signal, extract `### Purpose` from the snapshot already in memory (falling back to an author-labelled `**Objective:**` line when present, of which the corpus has one example [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/z_archive/022-sk-deep-research-evolution/005-sk-deep-research-review-mode/handover.md:33]), and render a `Purpose:` row in the RESUME BRIEF. No schema change, no migration, no new authoring.\n9. Candidate (f) is also the widest coverage per unit of effort, because every runtime's recovery surface already delegates to `/speckit:resume`: Claude's compaction output tells the session to run it [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/claude/session-prime.ts:135-170], dsh appends the same pointer [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/dsh/session-compact-context.mjs:122,126-142], and devin and pi emit `## Recovery\\nRun /speckit:resume …` [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/devin/post-compaction.cjs:266]. One read-side addition therefore reaches every hop the operator described, and it also fixes the misleading facet label from iteration 2 by making the real purpose available next to the next action.\n10. Validation plan for the recommendation: a fixture test in `resume-ladder.vitest.ts` with a packet whose `spec.md` Purpose is filled (assert the signal carries it and the brief renders the row), a second fixture whose only carrier is `goal.md` (assert the objective is used), and a negative fixture with neither (assert the row is omitted rather than rendering \"None\"); then one manual `/speckit:resume` against this packet and a check that the rendered brief shows the purpose line [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/tests/resume-ladder.vitest.ts].\n\n## Questions Answered\n- Q4: Which candidate change closes the measured gap at the lowest cost? — Answered by findings 1-10: the gap is on the read side, so candidates (a), (b) and (c) either fail the gap test or cost more for less; (d) is the most expensive and still partial; (e) is cheap but narrow; (f), one purpose row on the resume surface sourced from already-read text, closes the measured gap with a two-file, no-migration change.\n\n## Questions Remaining\n5.\n\n## Ruled Out\n- Parent-level acceptance criteria as the closing change: no purpose column, only 10 of 319 parents have the file, and enabling it touches four or more edit points.\n- A parent-level user story section as a standalone change: the parent template already has the purpose section, so the candidate i","file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/371-purpose-retention-research/specs/system-speckit/spec-authoring-and-continuity/006-purpose-retention-across-handovers/research/lineages/deepseek-1/iterations/iteration-004.md"},"truncated":true}
