{"type":"thinking","text":"Very important: the interactive `/speckit:resume` command workflow does NOT call buildResumeLadder. It instructs the model to read handover.md then `_memory.continuity` then spec docs, and renders a brief from reply_format (Confidence, Context source, Now, Last confirmed, Next safe action). The programmatic ladder exists and is consumed only by `authored-continuity-snapshot.ts:201` (Claude's opt-in compaction snapshot).\n\nThis materially adjusts Q4's recommendation: the smallest change consistent with evidence is either:\n(f1) add a Purpose row to the command workflow's step_2/step_3 instructions + reply_format (YAML, 1-2 files) — the model then reads spec.md's purpose as part of the ladder it's already told to walk; or\n(f2) add purpose to the programmatic ResumeSignal, which only changes the opt-in compaction snapshot (narrow coverage).\n\nThe cheapest with the widest coverage is (f1): the YAML already directs reading spec docs when handover/continuity are thin; adding \"Purpose\" to reply_format and a step instruction is a *prompt-level* change in 2 YAML assets (auto + confirm) + the presentation txt. That's 3 files, no code, no schema, no migration.\n\nHmm, which is truly cheapest? (f1) = 3 markdown/YAML files, no tests needed beyond a doc check; coverage = every /speckit:resume invocation in any runtime. (f2) = 2-3 code files + tests, coverage = only the opt-in snapshot.\n\nSo iteration 5 must revise the recommendation to (f1) with the correction documented — that's honest and valuable (\"what I would do differently\" + contradiction resolution). The lineage's final synthesis should carry the corrected recommendation. This is an \"insight\" iteration with high value: it corrects the previous recommendation with new evidence (contradictory-evidence handling).\n\nQ5 asks \"What is ruled out, and why?\" — and I can fold in this correction.\n\nIteration 5 findings:\n1. The interactive resume brief is produced by the command workflow's `reply_format` and step instructions, and that workflow never invokes the programmatic ladder: it tells the model to read handover.md, then `_memory.continuity`, then supporting spec docs [SOURCE: speckit-resume-auto.yaml:92-93,105-112,138,158-174]. The brief's fields are fixed at Confidence, Context source, Now, Last confirmed, Next safe action [SOURCE: :138].\n2. The programmatic ladder has exactly one consumer: `authored-continuity-snapshot.ts:201`, the Claude-only opt-in snapshot [SOURCE: authored-continuity-snapshot.ts:201; resume-ladder.ts:1058]. So a ResumeSignal change alone would only affect the opt-in compaction path, not the brief every operator sees — this corrects iteration 4's candidate (f) cost model.\n3. Corrected cheapest change: add a Purpose row to the resume workflow's reply_format/step_2 in both auto and confirm assets plus the presentation template (3 text files), sourcing it from the spec.md Purpose the workflow is already told to read when the canonical packet is thin. No code, no schema, no migration, and it reaches every runtime because every runtime's recovery pointer lands on /speckit:resume (iteration 2/4 evidence).\n4. Ruled out: parent-level acceptance criteria (no purpose column; 10/319; 4+ edit points) [iter1, iter4].\n5. Ruled out: parent-level user story section (template already has Purpose; validator exempt; no reader) [iter1, iter4].\n6. Ruled out: improved parent template as the closing change (same reason) [iter4].\n7. Ruled out: continuity-block purpose field as the *cheapest* (most expensive; migration; 29 handover-only packets) [iter2, iter4]; also ruled out as unnecessary given the read-side fix.\n8. Ruled out: compaction-brief purpose as the *primary* (narrow; per-runtime wiring; optional in Claude) [iter2, iter4].\n9. Ruled out (new, corpus-based): relying on authors to restate purpose in handovers (0/97 headings; 1/97 restatement) [iter3].\n10. Ruled out (new): the validator as the enforcement lever — the only executed required-anchor check exempts phase parents and the phase level's declared `problem` gate is never executed [iter1]; so \"make validation require the parent purpose\" is a different change from the one the evidence supports (it would enforce authoring, not reading), and it still would not cross a hop.\n\nResidual risks of the recommended change:\n- The brief is model-rendered from instructions, so the row's presence depends on model compliance; a YAML/reply_format line is a softer guarantee than code.\n- For phase children resumed via the parent, the parent's container purpose may be shown; the ladder's redirect goes to the child (iteration 2: followPhaseParentRedirect), so the child's purpose is the right one — but if the redirect fails, the parent's stale container purpose (38 vs 69) could mislead.\n- The purpose string could be long; the brief has no explicit budget (unlike the restore panel's 1200 chars), so a 481-char parent purpose would dominate the brief. Need a truncation rule.\n- Fixing the misleading \"Goal\" facet label (next_safe_action) is separate; the recommended change adds a true purpose next to it but doesn't relabel — residual confusion risk.\n\nThat's a strong Q5 + correction.\n\nnewInfoRatio: 3 fully new findings (1,2,3) + 6 partially new (rule-outs consolidated) + 1 synthesis; add +0.10. Compute: 10 findings, fully new 3, partially 6, redundant 1 → (3 + 3)/10 = 0.6 +0.1 = 0.70.\n\nEdge case: contradictory-evidence (iteration 4's recommendation vs the new consumer evidence). Yes.\n\nLet me write iteration 5, record, reduce. Then phase_synthesis: read all iterations, write research.md (progressive synthesis — I haven't created research.md yet! progressiveSynthesis true; config says research/research.md mutable. The lineage's research output path is {artifact_dir}/research.md (state_paths.research_output = {artifact_dir}/research.md). Hmm — the persona said research/research.md; the lineage layout uses research.md at the lineage root. The YAML state_paths.research_output = \"{artifact_dir}/research.md\". I'll write the synthesis to `research.md` in the lineage dir (matching the YAML), and also consider `research/research.md`? No — the lineage is the artifact_dir; `research.md` there is right.\n\nThen synthesis-closeout.cjs: run it as the YAML does with --artifact-dir {artifact_dir}, --state-log ... etc., --stop-reason maxIterationsReached, --answered-count, --total-questions. It produces events in an event dir which I then hand to the gateway. Then step_update_config_status: set status complete (config is immutable except this permitted flip). Then resource map emission (optional; config.resource_map.emit true → run reduce-state.cjs {spec_folder} --emit-resource-map? In fanout branch it's --emit-resource-map --fanout-resource-map-only at the packet level, which is outside my write surface (packet research/). Hmm: step_emit_resource_map writes {state_paths.resource_map_output} = {artifact_dir}/resource-map.md. In fanout mode, if_present runs reduce-state at the packet level with --fanout-resource-map-only — that writes the merged packet resource-map.md at research/resource-map.md, which is OUTSIDE my lineage. That's the orchestrator's/merge's job, not mine. I should not write outside. So I'll emit a lineage-local resource map if the script supports --artifact-dir with --emit-resource-map. Let me check reduce-state options. If it writes only to artifact dir, safe. I'll try `node reduce-state.cjs <spec> --artifact-dir <lineage> --emit-resource-map`.\n\nThen final synthesis record: what does synthesis-closeout emit? It emits synthesis_started/committed/complete events likely. The prompt says \"The terminal synthesis record must carry stopReason 'maxIterationsReached'\". synthesis-closeout takes --stop-reason. Let me inspect it before running.\n\nLet me first finish iteration 5."}
